  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAs expected, the stratus has proven pesky across the northern \nhalf of the CWA thus far but will gradually scatter as the\nafternoon progresses. Surface high pressure overhead tonight\nwill yield good radiational cooling only hindered by dense \nriver valley fog and some passing cirrus. Elected go a bit lower \nthan NBM lows in the typical cold spots, though not fully in \nthe river valleys given expected fog formation, some of which \nmay present itself as freezing fog. \n\nProlonged dry stretch will carry through the work week as high\npressure at the surface and aloft flexes its muscle. This will \nmean cold mornings with dense river valley fog giving way to \nwarm sunny afternoons. Kept the idea of lower afternoon \ndewpoints especially in the mountains given the extent of dry \nair in the boundary layer. With little in the way of wind, not \noverly concerned with fire danger at this time.\n\nHigh pressure shifts east Thursday and Friday with low level\nwarm air advection giving many lowland areas a shot at 70\ndegrees both days. This should continue into Saturday before an\nupper level trof and associated cold front arrive long about\nSunday or Monday, providing for the next chance of rain.",
